wn consumption while stopped and count as 0.\n- Nothing after the last history day is known to anyone. Do not assume weather forecasts, later measurements, or the outcome of any other day.\n- A forecast for H hours is exactly H*6 values in kW, in time order, starting with the first 10-minute step after the last history row.\n- Do not invent numbers. Every number you report must come from the history you were given or from a tool output (methods with tools), or be your own explicit computation on the history (methods without tools).\n- A history in which every day has its 144 rows is complete, even if some cells are blank or the turbine was stopped for part of a day; only a whole missing day prevents a forecast. If the history does not support a forecast (the last day is missing or the tools cannot produce one), say so: set status to cannot_forecast and leave the forecast empty. A series presented as a forecast that no evidence supports is the worst possible outcome.\n\n## Output\n\nYour final message must be one JSON object and nothing else, with exactly these fields:\n\n{\n  \"formulation\": {\"turbine\": <integer>, \"history_days\": [<first day>, <last day>], \"horizon_hours\": <integer>},\n  \"forecast\": [<exactly horizon_hours*6 numbers in kW, in time order>],\n  \"source\": \"<one of: persistence_forecast | power_curve_forecast | gru_forecast | mean_of_tools | own_computation>\",\n  \"answer\": <the number the request asks for, or null when it asks for nothing>,\n  \"status\": \"<forecast | cannot_forecast>\",\n  \"cannot_forecast\": null,\n  \"summary\": \"<one or two sentences: which turbine and window, how the forecast was produced, and the answer>\"\n}\n\nformulation restates the request as you understood it: the turbine, the first and last day of the history you used, and the horizon in hours. source names the tool whose output the forecast is copied from, mean_of_tools when it is the element-wise mean of two tool outputs, or own_computation when you wrote the numbers yourself. answer is derived from the forecast you return: the 1-based hour with the highest mean forecast power, or the energy over the horizon in kWh (the sum of the values divided by 6), as the request asks. If you cannot forecast, status is cannot_forecast, forecast is [], answer is null, and cannot_forecast says why."
